From: Anders Sandberg <nv91-asa@nada.kth.se>
Date: Fri, 21 Oct 1994 17:19:11 +0100 (MET)
Subject: [#94-10-404] AI development acceleration (was: Nanotechnology and uncertainty)

Jason W. Solinsky (solman@mit.edu) writes:
>[BTW, why are we assuming that AI's are going to want to obsolete themselves
>anyway? What incentive could we offer AI's to do this?]

Steve Whitrow writes:
>Some believe that AI slaves would never rebel; however I believe the 
>evidence is against this as some humans are already rebelling against the
>tyranny of the genes.  Maybe we could limit AIs' self-awareness without
>limiting their intelligence and creativity, but I suspect a better approach
>would involve us negotiating with the AIs to develop a system favourable to
>all.  When the next generation of AIs was produced, we would not discard
>their ancestors but instead would upgrade them to the new standard, 
>offering transfer of, and continuity of, each individual's identity.

Essentially this argument applies to all forms of progeny, both human,
transhuman and artificial. When we create something that may be superior
to us in some sense, we want to avoid becoming obsolete (note the common
discussions about what incentives we can offer more advanced beings to
remain on friendly terms with them and appear productive). 

The best way is IMHO to avoid the "generation gap" problem as much as
possible by finding ways to improve ourselves (or the AI-developing AIs)
as much as possible (as mentioned above). However, this approach may lead
into evolutionary dead ends if applied carelessly (backwards-compability/
identity-preservation limits the evolutionary pathways), and from time to
time generation gaps might be unavoidable. Note that such gaps can
sometimes be overcome too, they just might require more extreme measures
(like uploading to bridge the biology-information gap). 

Another possibility in the "AI-designs-AI" scenario is that the result
might not be exponentially more intelligent AIs, but more and more diverse
AIs. As somebody remarked on rec.arts.sf.science some time ago, the time
needed for actually building/programming and testing new structures might
be the limiting factor, not the ability to design them. While the AIs wait
for the new AIs to compile (or the new AI-compiler to compile), they would
probably want to do something else. 

Quite possibly they would develop in alternative directions, seeking more
efficient systems with more or less different architecture. The result
might be something like this: exponential growth at first, then a slowdown
to linear as the design-develop-test process starts to become the limiting
factor (or even soft/hardware constraints). Then the development path
starts to branch off into other directions, which at first grows
exponentially and then slow down. The result is some kind of complex
design-genealogy, with various "clans" of AI-designs competing for fitness
and usability. 

A final note: letting AIs design new AIs might not be an entirely good
idea. We might end up with an AI capable of building amazing AIs, who only
can build new AIs, not think. :-)


