From: nancy@genie.slhs.udel.edu
Date:     Fri, 21 Oct 94 16:56:41 GMT
Subject: [#94-10-406] Autonomy: Threat or Menace?

 Fred Hapgood <fhapgood@world.std.com> wrote: 
>
>From: nancy@genie.slhs.udel.edu
>
> Fred Hapgood <fhapgood@world.std.com> wrote: 
>
>FH> It is very hard to imagine anybody even wanting to *buy* a
>    machine that spontaneously, unpredictably, and autonomously
>    modified its behavior in any deep sense.
>
>NL> I've heard that there's still an active slave trade, mostly
>    in Islamic parts of the world.  In any case, it's certainly
>    true that people paid quite a bit for slaves in the past.
> 
>Well, exactly.  And look how hard slave-owning societies work to
>make sure their slaves stick exactly to the defined routines.
>*Nothing* is scarier to a slave-owning society than an
>autonomous, self-modifying slave.  Slave-owning societies are
>famously hostile to technological change (at the time of the CW
>the South had exactly *one* railroad line) for just this reason:
>any change at all might compromise the severity and
>relentlessness of their control.
>
This is a pretty good argument for giving rights to AIs instead of
owning them. 

>Truth is, the AI problem is deeper than regulators concerned
>about the second- and third- order unintended consequences for
>public safety coming from self-modifying machines.  It's deeper
>than the marketing problem of consumers worried about wasting
>their investment when their backhoe goes green and refuses to
>disrupt the balance of subsurface microbial ecologies.

The risk is actually comparable to having an employee go green
and leave. It's a pain, and it might represent the loss of a 
fair amount of training cost, but companies live with it.
And, of course, the backhoe might be *right*. 

I don't think that AIs are going to be any more arbitrary than
people are--they'll have constraints (desire for self-preservation
and prisoners' dilemna issues) that will mostly leave them
somewhat predictable and buyable.

If I want to worry about AIs, it will be that they probably
won't have the sort of empathy which I believe is a major
inhibitor against human violence. (It apparently takes a
lot of pressure to get most humans to kill their own kind,
and most people find it seriously painful to do so.) 

Of course, AIs might worry that humans have generally *no*
inhibitions against killing/modifying AIs.

>Suppose there was this commune of socially irresponsible, rich,
>technically sophisticated cognitive artists.  Because they are
>socially irresponsible they don't care about the public safety
>issues; because they are rich, they don't care about the
>marketing problems; because they are technically skilled, they
>can build anything they can define; and because they are artists,
>they need have no other motive for building AI than 'leading
>society to question the meaning of the person/machine boundary'
>or something.

Suppose there was a commune of bright rich people who wanted
to play at biological warfare?

Probably the only thing that saves us is that intelligence isn't
all that inheritable. :-) (The people who earned their fortunes
probably won't have been as insulated and probably won't be
as irresponsible.)

Suppose there is a non-communal group of irresponsible rich
people whose idea of how to spend their lives is to fight
each other over "their" territories--in which a number of
people who aren't in the group just happen to be living?
This one has already killed tens of millions of people,
and I'm a lot more worried about it than about anything
that can be accomplished by a small consensual group.

Actually, this sounds a lot like the amazing non-existant
mad scientist. So far as I've been able to find out, the
pulp fiction "mad scientist" who develops major new devices
and uses them for malicious purposes has never existed....
of course, there have been a few crazy scientists, but that's
a different issue.

Maybe this kind of thing could come to be a problem as people
get access to more material and informational resources, but
I don't think that we're anywhere near there yet....my impression
is that scientists have trouble getting the money to do even
fairly small projects, and they don't seem to be able to
save it out of their salaries. One of my fantasies is a world
where it's simply no big deal for people to be able to save
enough to take a year off to do research--I suspect that this
alone would be enough to accelerate invention considerably.

Nanotechnologists worry about the invention of gray goo--I bet
that a generalized gray goo would be *much* harder to invent
than they're allowing for, though I'd be interested in any
thoughts on the subject. (Even a less-than-perfect gray goo
could be serious--say something that ate plastic, though.) 

>OK.  So here they are at the preliminary design conference.
>First on the agenda is defining exactly what is meant by
>artificial autonomy, or superhuman intelligence, or whatever, for
>the purposes of actual design.  What do they say?  What do they
>do?

I have a little bet with myself that superhuman intelligence
will have to be evolved as much as designed.

Is the net an example of artificial autonomy? It certainly
isn't under anyone's control.

>The fundamental problem with building a machine that has
>'superhuman intelligence' is that the term itself has no
>programmatic content. Even the term 'learning' is looking
>problematical -- lots of people working in AI today refuse to
>even participate in discusions over what learning means. They
>call it 'the L-word' and make the sign of the cross if you try to
>bring it up.

Interesting--I should take a look at what they're saying about
it. Offhand, I wonder if what's happened is that scientists  has
considered mental events unmanagably vague/unreal/unproven/unprovable 
for so long that they have trouble thinking about them. 

>I suggest we just concentrate on making a really good Go program.
>That's what *I* mean by superhuman intelligence.

Do you mean a program which plays better than humans, or that
it would take superhuman intelligence to write one? :-)

It's an interesting question--how *would* you recognize superhuman
intelligence? If it's just moderately superhuman, then it does
human activities better than anyone else does, but if it's
extremely superhuman, then how would we know? 

Nancy Lebovitz

