CLOSING THE "VALUES-GAP": 
A Call to Human Intelligence
By Vigdor Schreibman

     All our chronic problems combined--massive public debt; increasingly
unfair distribution of wealth; racial strife; ungovernable violent crime;
family breakdown; urban disintegration; and global eco-blindness--are merely
symptoms of a larger predicament that may be basic to human nature.  The
ultimate challenge to humanity now coming into view is the critical need to
overcome our own instinctual limitations.  As one emeritus scientist pointed
out in 1990, "the mass of individuals who make up society have inherited
'evolution's fatal flaw'--a built-in instinct that calls for survival and
reproduction in the here-and-now, with no instinctual concern for survival
of the species in the long term" [Potter, 1990] [Fins-PaN-13].  The ominous
significance of that dilemma, previously disregarded by humanity, is looming
clearer every day. Edwin Dobb, senior editor of The Sciences, wrote this
warning in 1989:
                        
     Earth system scientists have predicted that, as a direct conse-
     quence of increased carbon dioxide levels and resultant greenhouse
     effect, storms will become more numerous in the coming decades, and
     they will be far more severe, with winds in excess of two hundred
     miles per hour (high atmospheric temperatures will accelerate
     evaporation, which will speed up atmospheric convection currents). 
     Droughts will become common in the middle latitudes; rivers in the
     American Southwest, for example, may shrink by as much as forty to
     seventy-five percent, all but obliterating agriculture.  And polar
     ice will begin melting, causing sea levels to rise by as much as
     two hundred feet and submerging such highly populated costal areas
     as Hong Kong, New York, and Rio de Janeiro.  The specter raised by
     such changes, at least in the minds of some scientists, is that man
     constitutes a threat to the global processes that, until now, have
     maintained the conditions necessary for life. 
 
     Neurobiologist William H. Calvin places that threat in the perspective
of human nature when he observes, "All of this rapid change during our lives
has led to a boom-time psychology and physiology that entices us to exploit 
the opportunities of the moment rather than investing in the future.  This
opportunistic aspect of our basic biological heritage, shaped by the ice-age
population explosions, is winning out over the predicting-the-future
abilities that might avoid the bust that usually follows the ... boom."
 
     It is "simply reckless" to continue in the current opportunistic mode,
Calvin also observes.  Abrupt climate change of only a few degrees is the
kind of event that flipped Europe into its last cold spike for 800 years (the
"Younger Dryas" cold spike), then suddenly warmed again.  Roughly 20 such
cold spikes have occurred during the last 120,000 years, according to the
published records of the Greenland ice cores, evidencing "40 sudden changes,
either sudden cooling or sudden warming, every 3,000 years on average."  Most
significant Calvin writes, such abrupt climate change "could happen tomorrow,
at the rate we're jostling the system via burning fossil fuels, cutting
forests, producing methane and refrigerator gases."  The seemingly irresist-
able doubling of global population to 10 billion people within the next 50
years, could place unsustainable pressures on the fragile biosphere. 

     Craig Dorman, director of the Woods Hole (Mass.) Oceanographic Institu-
tion issued a call to researchers in 1992, to mobilize "almost on a war
footing" to meet the challenge of "global and national needs for knowledge
about the environment and man's effect on it." In order to check the increase
in the human population and to preserve and restore the natural environment,
a number of prominent scientists and scientific societies have called for
massive changes in societal behavior through conscious cultural evolution and
environmental revolution [Potter and Grantham, 1992; Malone, 1994]
[Fins-PaN-13].

     An environmental education Mission Model can be developed for those
purposes, to assure the availability of a coherent and purposeful learning
system, as educators have previously shown [UVA-Warfield, 1979]. Moreover,
the emerging global information infrastruture can be developed to facilitate
individuals and organizations in overcoming "evolution's fatal flaw," by
systematic design and deployment of a viable theory of action, of the kind
that state-of-the-art management and design science experts are now utilizing
[Argyris, 1982; Warfield, 1990; Senge, 1990].   We can, thereby, transform
the dominant opportunistic perception toward society and the environment into
a collaborative vision to build a desirable future.  In the spirit of
realizing that vision FINS will publish a two-part paper on the topic,
"Desirable Information Technology: To sustain and enhance human development"
[Fins-PaN-11,12]. A series of special reports on the leaders of this global
initiative will also be published by FINS in the coming days and weeks.

     Next Thursday October 27, 1994, the President's Council on Sustainable
Development will meet in Washington, DC to consider this situation.  (vphone
202-408-5296; fax 202-408-6839).   Now is the time for a call to the innate
intellectual capability of human beings, including the President's Council,
to secure the survival of the biosphere of Planet Earth by massive changes
in societal behavior facilitated by "Desirable Information Technology." 

